From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Brandon McGinty <brandon.mcginty@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 2 Oct 2026 19:21:42 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 03/12] speakup: carry Unicode scalar values through the speech
 path

Speakup held every character in a `u16`, so Speakup could represent no
code point above U+FFFF.  A Unicode scalar value needs 21 bits, so a
supplementary-plane character such as U+20000 did not fit.

Widen every variable, array and function signature that holds a code
point from `u16` to `u32`:

  buffers.c      synth_buffer, synth_buffer_add, synth_buffer_getc,
                 synth_buffer_peek
  synth.c        the _spk_do_catch_up character, synth_putwc,
                 synth_putwc_s
  main.c         buf, sentbuf, sentmarks, sentbufend, spk_lastkey,
                 get_char, speak_char, spkup_write, update_color_buffer,
                 speakup_con_write and the per-function character locals
  spk_types.h    synth_out_unicode, ht.highbuf
  devsynth.c     the speakup_file_writeu conversion buffer
  speakup_soft.c the softsynthx_read character

A screen pointer and a screen word stay `u16`, because the virtual
terminal stores one `u16` per screen cell.  `get_char` therefore keeps a
`u16 *` parameter while returning `u32`.

`synth_buffer_skip_nonlatin1` keeps its `< 0x100` test, so a hardware
synthesizer and `/dev/softsynth` still receive Latin-1 only.

`speakup_con_write` takes `const u32 *` and `spkup_write` keeps its
loop, so the only other change at the call site is the type of the
`vt_notifier_call` local that holds the character.

This commit widens storage only.  No code point above U+FFFF reaches
the buffer yet, because `synth_writeu` and `speakup_file_writeu` still
discard one.

Signed-off-by: Brandon McGinty <brandon.mcginty@gmail.com>
